  "phaseMenstrualName": "Menstrual period",
  "phaseMenstrualShortName": "Period",
  "phaseMenstrualDescription": "A new cycle begins on the first day of bleeding. As hormone levels drop, the uterine lining that thickened during the previous cycle sheds and leaves the body through the vagina. A period usually lasts 2–7 days, and a total blood loss of about 30–80 ml is within the normal range.",
  "phaseMenstrualSymptomNote": "The uterus contracts to push the lining out, so abdominal and back pain are common, especially in the first 1–2 days. Symptoms gradually ease as the period ends.",
  "phaseMenstrualTip1Title": "Apply warmth to your belly",
  "phaseMenstrualTip1Subtitle": "Helps the uterine muscles relax and eases cramping.",
  "phaseMenstrualTip2Title": "Get more iron",
  "phaseMenstrualTip2Subtitle": "Red meat, dark leafy greens and beans help replace lost iron.",
  "phaseMenstrualTip3Title": "Rest more",
  "phaseMenstrualTip3Subtitle": "Favor gentle activity like walking and stretching.",
  "phaseFollicularName": "Follicular phase",
  "phaseFollicularShortName": "Follicular",
  "phaseFollicularDescription": "The hormone FSH stimulates follicles in the ovaries to develop, and one dominant follicle continues to mature. Rising estrogen helps the uterine lining thicken again. The chance of conception is still low now but increases as the fertile window approaches.",
  "phaseFollicularSymptomNote": "Rising estrogen often makes you feel more alert, cheerful and energetic. It's a good time for high-intensity workouts or starting something new.",
  "phaseFollicularTip1Title": "Train hard",
  "phaseFollicularTip1Subtitle": "Your body recovers well — great for cardio and weight training.",
  "phaseFollicularTip2Title": "Eat plenty of greens",
  "phaseFollicularTip2Subtitle": "Fiber helps your body metabolize estrogen.",
  "phaseFertileName": "Fertile window",
  "phaseFertileShortName": "Fertile",
  "phaseFertileDescription": "Estrogen is high, and cervical mucus becomes clear and stretchy, helping sperm move more easily. Sperm can survive up to 5 days in the body, so unprotected sex on these days can lead to pregnancy.",
  "phaseFertileSymptomNote": "Egg-white-like mucus is a natural sign that your body is about to ovulate. Tracking it helps pinpoint your fertile window more accurately.",
  "phaseFertileTip1Title": "Use protection",
  "phaseFertileTip1Subtitle": "Use contraception if you aren't planning a pregnancy.",
  "phaseFertileTip2Title": "Take your morning temperature",
  "phaseFertileTip2Subtitle": "Helps confirm ovulation after it has happened.",
  "phaseOvulationName": "Ovulation day",
  "phaseOvulationShortName": "Ovulation",
  "phaseOvulationDescription": "The LH surge causes the dominant follicle to rupture and release an egg into the fallopian tube. The egg survives only about 12–24 hours, so this is the most fertile time of the cycle. The ovulation day is an estimate and may be off by a few days.",
  "phaseOvulationSymptomNote": "Some people feel a mild, sharp pain on one side of the lower abdomen during ovulation. It usually lasts from a few minutes to a few hours and is normal.",
  "phaseOvulationTip1Title": "Your most fertile time",
  "phaseOvulationTip1Subtitle": "Useful if you're planning a pregnancy.",
  "phaseOvulationTip2Title": "Stay hydrated",
  "phaseOvulationTip2Subtitle": "Helps reduce bloating and discomfort around ovulation.",
  "phaseLutealName": "Luteal phase",
  "phaseLutealShortName": "Luteal",
  "phaseLutealDescription": "After ovulation, the follicle turns into the corpus luteum and releases progesterone to prepare the uterine lining for implantation. Body temperature rises slightly, by about 0.3–0.5°C. The chance of conception drops quickly after ovulation.",
  "phaseLutealSymptomNote": "Rising progesterone can make you hungrier and sleepier. Eating smaller, more frequent meals and getting enough sleep will help you feel better.",
  "phaseLutealTip1Title": "Exercise gently",
  "phaseLutealTip1Subtitle": "Yoga, walking and swimming suit your gradually lower energy.",
  "phaseLutealTip2Title": "Eat small, frequent meals",
  "phaseLutealTip2Subtitle": "Keeps blood sugar steady and curbs sugar cravings.",
  "phasePmsShortName": "Premenstrual",
  "phasePmsDescription": "If the egg isn't fertilized, the corpus luteum breaks down and estrogen and progesterone levels fall. This drop can cause premenstrual syndrome (PMS). Your next period begins when the uterine lining sheds.",
  "phasePmsSymptomNote": "PMS usually appears in the 5 days or so before your period and goes away once it starts. If symptoms significantly affect your daily life, talk to a doctor.",
  "phasePmsTip1Title": "Cut back on salt and caffeine",
  "phasePmsTip1Subtitle": "Helps reduce bloating, breast tenderness and irritability.",
  "phasePmsTip2Title": "Be prepared",
  "phasePmsTip2Subtitle": "Carry pads in case your period comes early.",
  "phaseLateName": "Late period",
  "phaseLateShortName": "Late",
  "phaseLateDescription": "Your period is later than expected. Being a few days late is quite common and can be caused by stress or changes in sleep, weight or routine. If you've had unprotected sex, you can take a pregnancy test for peace of mind.",
  "phaseLateSymptomNote": "These symptoms may be prolonged PMS or early signs of pregnancy. If your period is more than 1 week late, or this happens several months in a row, see a doctor.",
  "phaseLateTip1Title": "Double-check your calendar",
  "phaseLateTip1Subtitle": "You may have forgotten to log the start of your period.",
  "phaseLateTip2Title": "Consider a pregnancy test",
  "phaseLateTip2Subtitle": "If you've had unprotected sex this cycle.",
